  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.25 × loss. It is 3 times steeper across the screen (x), so overshooting shows there first. Above a learning rate of 2/3 the x swings grow, so the run diverges.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with spread 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, so it is exactly 0 at 64. It comes from a fixed seed in one sequence, and a new run continues that sequence.",
    "Purple arrow: the next mini-batch step, the way the ball will move. Dark arrow: the step the full data would give. Both are drawn at 1.5 times their true length, one scale for both. Arrows shorter than 0.12 are hidden, and the readout still shows the length.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. It is diverged when the loss goes above 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The ball and path are then held at the rim, and the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. Small batches can raise it on single steps. The run moves 5 steps per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}